\title{Weighted Homology as the Quantitative Bridge Between\\Topological Quantum Codes and Combinatorial Systoles}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 20, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Topological quantum error correction is often summarized by saying that logical operators are noncontractible cycles and code distance is the length of a shortest such cycle. This statement is correct only after the relevant identification is shown to preserve support size. We develop a finite, model-independent framework that makes this requirement explicit. A weighted homology model is a finite pointed class space equipped with a natural-number weight, and its systole is the minimum weight among nontrivial classes. We prove that pointed weight-preserving equivalences preserve nontriviality and systole, and derive the distance--systole correspondence for any homological code whose logical and geometric sectors are related by such an equivalence. If distance equals systole, squared systole is bounded by a constant times combinatorial area, and area is bounded linearly in genus, then squared distance is bounded linearly in genus. The standard square torus satisfies the exact identity $2d^2=E$, where $E$ is its edge count. Conversely, genus alone cannot bound distance, because arbitrarily fine square cellulations of a genus-one torus have unbounded distance. We also discuss homotopy compatibility, finite algorithms, surface and color codes, higher dimensions, and speculative codes derived from algebraic varieties.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

A topological quantum code stores logical information in global degrees of freedom of a cellulated space. Physical errors are local, while nontrivial logical operators must extend across the geometry. On a surface, an undetectable logical operator is often represented by an essential cycle, meaning a loop that is not homologous to zero. Its minimum support is the code distance.

The familiar slogan
\[
  \text{code distance}=\text{shortest essential cycle}
\]
contains more information than an abstract homology isomorphism. Homology records which cycles belong to the same class, whereas distance records the minimum number of physical sites in a representative. An isomorphism can preserve addition and the zero class while permuting short and long classes. The correct bridge must therefore preserve \emph{weighted} classes.

We isolate the finite minimum theory needed for this bridge. At the level of distance, only a finite class set, a distinguished trivial class, a weight function, and at least one nontrivial class are needed. The first results establish existence and attainment of the minimum nonzero weight. The central invariance theorem shows that a pointed weight-preserving bijection leaves this minimum unchanged. It follows that logical distance equals geometric systole whenever logical operators and homology classes are identified in this quantitative sense.

We then distinguish qualitative topology from quantitative geometry. Homotopy-equivalent realizations preserve fundamental groups, but they need not preserve combinatorial lengths. Genus counts handles but does not control cellulation scale. We consequently state the precise assumptions behind square-root genus scaling and exhibit a genus-one obstruction to every unnormalized genus-only bound.

\section{Finite weighted homology}

\begin{definition}[Finite weighted homology model]
A finite weighted homology model is a triple
\[
  \mathcal H=(H,0_H,w_H),
\]
where $H$ is a finite set, $0_H\in H$ is a distinguished trivial class, and $w_H:H\to\mathbb N$ is a weight function. The model is \emph{nontrivial} if some $x\in H$ satisfies $x\ne0_H$.
\end{definition}

In a cellular model, $H$ may be a homology group and $w_H(x)$ the minimum support of a cycle representing $x$. In a code model, $H$ may consist of logical operator classes modulo stabilizers and $w_H(x)$ the minimum number of physical qubits touched by a representative. No algebraic operation is required for the minimum arguments, although standard examples carry one.

\begin{definition}[Nonzero spectrum and systole]
For a nontrivial finite weighted model $\mathcal H$, define
\[
 W^\times(\mathcal H)=\{w_H(x):x\in H,\ x\ne0_H\}.
\]
The combinatorial systole is
\[
 \operatorname{sys}(\mathcal H)=\min W^\times(\mathcal H).
\]
When weight counts one-cells in a least representative, this is the combinatorial one-systole.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Lower-bound property]
For every nonzero $x\in H$,
\[
 \operatorname{sys}(\mathcal H)\le w_H(x).
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The number $w_H(x)$ belongs to the finite nonempty set $W^\times(\mathcal H)$, whose minimum is no larger than any member.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Attainment]
If $\mathcal H$ is nontrivial, there exists $x_*\ne0_H$ such that
\[
 w_H(x_*)=\operatorname{sys}(\mathcal H).
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The set of nonzero classes is finite and nonempty. Its image under $w_H$ is finite and nonempty, so the minimum belongs to that image. Any preimage of the minimum is suitable.
\end{proof}

Thus the finite systole is a genuine extremum, not merely a limiting value. These two elementary properties are the engine of the transport theorem.

\section{Weighted equivalence and invariance}

\begin{definition}[Pointed weight-preserving equivalence]
For finite weighted models $\mathcal H=(H,0_H,w_H)$ and $\mathcal K=(K,0_K,w_K)$, a pointed weight-preserving equivalence is a bijection $f:H\to K$ such that
\[
 f(0_H)=0_K,\qquad w_K(f(x))=w_H(x)\quad\text{for every }x\in H.
\]
We also call $f$ a weighted isometry.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Preservation of nontriviality]
A pointed weight-preserving equivalence $f:\mathcal H\to\mathcal K$ exists only between models that are either both trivial or both nontrivial.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
If $x\ne0_H$, then $f(x)\ne0_K$; otherwise injectivity and $f(0_H)=0_K$ imply $x=0_H$. The converse follows by applying the same argument to $f^{-1}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Systole Invariance Theorem]
Let $f:\mathcal H\to\mathcal K$ be a pointed weight-preserving equivalence between nontrivial finite weighted models. Then
\[
 \operatorname{sys}(\mathcal H)=\operatorname{sys}(\mathcal K).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Choose $x_*\ne0_H$ attaining $\operatorname{sys}(\mathcal H)$. Its image is nonzero and has equal weight, so
\[
 \operatorname{sys}(\mathcal K)\le w_K(f(x_*))
 =w_H(x_*)=\operatorname{sys}(\mathcal H).
\]
Applying the same argument to $f^{-1}$ gives the reverse inequality.
\end{proof}

An unweighted isomorphism is insufficient. Equal Betti numbers record only dimensions. Even a group isomorphism protects algebraic structure rather than support cost. If an isomorphism sends a short class to a long one, it cannot transport a claim about distance. More generally, if
\[
 a\,w_H(x)\le w_K(f(x))\le b\,w_H(x)
\]
for positive constants $a,b$, then the same minimizer argument gives corresponding two-sided systolic inequalities. Exact invariance is the case $a=b=1$.

\section{Homological quantum codes}

\begin{definition}[Finite homological code]
A finite homological code consists of a nontrivial finite weighted logical model $\mathcal L=(L,0_L,w_L)$, a finite weighted geometric model $\mathcal H=(H,0_H,w_H)$, and a pointed weight-preserving equivalence $\Phi:\mathcal L\to\mathcal H$. Its distance is
\[
 d(C)=\min\{w_L(\ell):\ell\in L,\ \ell\ne0_L\}.
\]
\end{definition}

Stabilizer presentations, coefficient fields, and cell dimensions determine the ingredients in concrete constructions. The abstract result concerns their quantitative consequence once the logical--geometric dictionary has been established.

\begin{theorem}[Distance--Systole Correspondence]
For every finite homological code, the geometric model is nontrivial and
\[
 d(C)=\operatorname{sys}(\mathcal H).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By definition, $d(C)=\operatorname{sys}(\mathcal L)$. Nontriviality transfers through $\Phi$, and the Systole Invariance Theorem gives $\operatorname{sys}(\mathcal L)=\operatorname{sys}(\mathcal H)$.
\end{proof}

For a surface code with qubits on edges, products of operators along cycles give logical classes modulo local boundaries or coboundaries. If this quotient is identified with first homology and support agrees with the minimum edge count of representatives, the theorem identifies the sector distance with the one-systole. A CSS code generally has primal and dual sectors; the argument applies separately to both, and total distance is the smaller sector minimum.

Color codes have related homological descriptions. Here too, a vector-space isomorphism alone does not settle distance. One must identify the correct class space, perhaps the first homology of an associated flag complex, and prove exact support preservation or a controlled distortion estimate.

\section{Geometry, genus, and square-root scaling}

\begin{theorem}[Square-Root Genus Transfer Theorem]
Let $d,s,A,g,\alpha,\beta$ be nonnegative integers. If
\[
 d=s,\qquad s^2\le\alpha A,\qquad A\le\beta g,
\]
then
\[
 d^2\le\alpha\beta g.
\]
Consequently, as a real inequality,
\[
 d\le\sqrt{\alpha\beta}\,\sqrt g.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Substitution and monotonicity give
\[
 d^2=s^2\le\alpha A\le\alpha\beta g.
\]
Both sides are nonnegative, so taking square roots proves the second claim.
\end{proof}

The hypotheses have distinct roles. The first is the coding-to-geometry bridge. The second is a geometric systolic estimate. The third normalizes cellulation size relative to topology. The explicit constants prevent an unjustified asymptotic assertion.

Consider the standard $n\times n$ square cellulation of a torus. It has $n^2$ horizontal and $n^2$ vertical edges, hence $E=2n^2$. A shortest essential horizontal or vertical cycle contains $n$ edges and, under the standard support identification, $d=n$.

\begin{proposition}[Square Torus Distance--Area Identity]
If a square torus has distance $d=n$ and edge count $E=2n^2$, then
\[
 2d^2=E.
\]
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Substitute $d=n$ into the left-hand side.
\end{proof}

Thus $d=\sqrt{E/2}$ exactly. For $n=2,3,5,8$, the pairs $(E,d)$ are $(8,2)$, $(18,3)$, $(50,5)$, and $(128,8)$.

\begin{theorem}[No Genus-Only Distance Bound]
For every nonnegative integer $B$, there exist surface-code parameters $d$ and $g$ with
\[
 g=1\qquad\text{and}\qquad B<d.
\]
Hence no finite bound depending only on genus controls distance across arbitrary refinements.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Choose the square torus with $n=B+1$. Its genus is $1$, while its shortest essential grid cycle and corresponding distance equal $B+1>B$.
\end{proof}

This does not conflict with the transfer theorem. In the refinement family, the area $E=2n^2$ is unbounded at fixed genus, so no uniform $\beta$ can satisfy $E\le\beta g$. A meaningful $O(\sqrt g)$ claim concerns normalized, bounded-geometry families with area at most linear in genus.

\section{Homotopy compatibility and metric instability}

\begin{theorem}[Fundamental-Group Compatibility]
If topological spaces $X$ and $Y$ are homotopy equivalent, then for every basepoint $x\in X$ there is a corresponding basepoint $y\in Y$ such that
\[
 \pi_1(X,x)\cong\pi_1(Y,y).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
A homotopy equivalence has a homotopy inverse. Their induced maps on fundamental groups compose to identity maps after the standard basepoint transport, and hence are mutually inverse group homomorphisms.
\end{proof}

This theorem supports cellulation-independent construction of qualitative loop sectors. Abelianization and a coefficient choice may then produce first homology. However, homotopy equivalence does not preserve combinatorial support. A subdivision can replace a single edge by a long path while retaining the same homotopy type. Fundamental groups and homology may remain isomorphic while systoles differ by an arbitrarily large factor. Topological invariance must therefore be supplemented by weighted isometry or controlled distortion.

\section{Algorithms}

\begin{algorithm}[Finite Nontrivial Minimum]
Given a finite class list, a distinguished zero class, and a weight table: initialize an undefined current minimum; scan every class; skip the zero class; update the minimum and a witnessing class whenever a smaller weight appears; report triviality if no nonzero class appears; otherwise return the minimum and witness.
\end{algorithm}

For $N=|H|$, this uses $O(N)$ weight evaluations and $O(1)$ auxiliary storage beyond the input. It is optimal in the comparison model, since an unseen class could have smaller weight.

\begin{algorithm}[Pointed Weighted-Isometry Test]
Given explicit finite models and a candidate map $f:H\to K$: verify $f(0_H)=0_K$; insert all images into a set and reject duplicates; check that every member of $K$ occurs and $|H|=|K|$; then verify $w_K(f(x))=w_H(x)$ for every $x\in H$. Accept exactly when all tests pass.
\end{algorithm}

For hashable classes and constant-time weight access, expected time is $O(|H|+|K|)$ and space is $O(|K|)$. A sorting implementation takes $O(N\log N)$ time.

\begin{algorithm}[Genus-Bound Certificate]
Given $d,s,A,g,\alpha,\beta$, check $d=s$, $s^2\le\alpha A$, and $A\le\beta g$. If they hold, return the certified bound $d^2\le\alpha\beta g$ and slack $\alpha\beta g-d^2$; otherwise identify the failed hypothesis.
\end{algorithm}

This checker validates an instance of the transfer theorem. It does not discover a geometric systolic inequality. Its arithmetic cost is polynomial in the bit lengths of the six integers.

\section{Applications and interpretation}

The results suggest a modular design strategy. First classify logical operators. Second identify them with geometric cycles. Third prove that the identification preserves minimal support. Fourth establish a systolic inequality for the cellulation family. Finally normalize area against genus or another parameter. This sequence prevents a statement about the number of classes from being mistaken for a statement about representative length.

A speculative arithmetic program might begin with a smooth projective variety, choose a finite cellular or simplicial model, and use a homology group to label sectors. Several further steps are essential: one must specify a physical-qubit architecture, define stabilizers and logical equivalence, connect geometric and logical classes, identify representative size with operator support, and show that reduction or specialization controls weights rather than merely homology ranks. The present theorems do not assert that every variety supplies these data. They identify a sufficient interface. If weighted spectra persist across reductions up to controlled scaling, code distances inherit the same control.

For cellular CSS codes on a closed $D$-manifold, logical operators may arise from $k$-cycles and complementary dual cycles. The finite weighted-isometry argument is dimension-free and applies to $k$-dimensional support. The new work in higher dimensions is geometric: one needs duality-compatible identifications and inequalities for higher-dimensional systoles.

\section{Discussion, limitations, and future directions}

The framework gives exact conclusions from exact hypotheses, while deliberately leaving construction-specific geometry outside the abstract theorem. It does not derive cellulations from varieties, prove that arbitrary homology classes act as logical operators, or supply universal systolic inequalities.

Three cautions are central. First, finite weighted homology is stronger than finite homology: equal class counts and equal algebraic structure do not imply equal weight spectra. Second, homotopy invariance protects class structure rather than combinatorial length. Uniformly bounded local subdivision degree may control distortion, while unrestricted refinement does not. Third, genus scaling requires size normalization; the torus refinement family already defeats every genus-only bound.

Future research should investigate arithmetic persistence of complete minimum-weight spectra under good reduction, rather than persistence of Betti numbers alone. Higher-dimensional codes call for distance--systole duality in complementary dimensions, natural under bounded-degree subdivisions. For closed orientable surfaces with bounded face and vertex degrees and edge count linear in genus, the sharp target is $d\le C\sqrt g$ together with infinite families satisfying $d\ge c\sqrt g$. Color codes motivate a support-controlled equivalence with the first homology of a flag complex, possibly with universal distortion factor two. Finally, pairs of homotopy-equivalent finite complexes with equal fundamental groups and mod-two homology but widely separated systoles can clarify exactly which local geometric conditions guarantee metric stability.

\section{Conclusion}

The quantitative invariant behind topological quantum-code distance is not homology dimension alone. It is the minimum nonzero value in a weighted homology spectrum. A pointed weight-preserving equivalence makes logical and geometric minima agree, turning code distance into the combinatorial systole.

This transport principle separates coding theory from geometry. Support preservation supplies the bridge, systolic inequalities supply distance bounds, and area normalization supplies the missing condition for square-root genus scaling. The square torus illustrates both conclusions: it satisfies $2d^2=E$, yet arbitrary refinements at genus one rule out every bound based on genus alone. Future constructions must therefore classify the classes, retain their weights, and control the maps between them. Topology determines which logical loops exist; weighted geometry determines how well they protect information.

\end{document}

